---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is a URL slug?",
    answer: "A URL slug is the user-readable section at the very end of a web address (for example, 'url-slug-generator' in 'creatorsfeel.com/tools/url-slug-generator'). It identifies a specific page using descriptive words rather than database IDs or arbitrary query parameters."
  },
  {
    question: "Why are hyphens preferred over underscores in URL slugs?",
    answer: "Search engines like Google specifically treat hyphens (-) as word separators (reading 'url-slug' as two distinct words: 'url' and 'slug'). Conversely, underscores (_) are treated as character joiners (reading 'url_slug' as a single compound string 'urlslug'). Using hyphens ensures clear readability and proper keyword recognition."
  },
  {
    question: "Should I remove stop words from every URL slug?",
    answer: "Not necessarily. Removing common stop words like 'a', 'the', 'in', 'to', and 'for' helps keep URLs short, focused, and punchy. However, you should retain stop words whenever omitting them alters the intended meaning or grammatical clarity of your title (such as 'room-for-rent' or 'to-be-or-not-to-be')."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the recommended length for an SEO-friendly URL slug?",
    answer: "The ideal length for a URL slug is between 3 to 5 words, or roughly 30 to 60 characters. Keeping slugs concise prevents truncation in mobile browser address bars and search engine results pages (SERPs)."
  },
  {
    question: "Can URL slugs contain uppercase letters?",
    answer: "No, URL slugs should always be strictly lowercase. Web servers running on Linux or Unix treat uppercase and lowercase characters as separate URLs. Using mixed casing can cause 404 broken links, duplicate content penalties, and canonical confusion."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I change a URL slug after publishing a page?",
    answer: "Yes, but doing so alters the page address. If your page is already indexed or has incoming backlinks from other websites, you must set up a permanent 301 redirect from the old URL to the new URL to preserve your search equity and avoid broken links."
  },
  {
    question: "What characters are not permitted in a clean URL slug?",
    answer: "URL slugs should never contain spaces, punctuation marks, emojis, or reserved URL symbols such as '?', '#', '%', '&', '+', and '@'. These symbols either break URL routing or get percent-encoded into messy, unreadable strings like '%20'."
  },
  {
    question: "Does having keywords in a URL slug guarantee higher Google rankings?",
    answer: "No. While including your primary keyword in the URL slug provides a helpful relevance signal to search engines and reassures users about page content, it is only one minor factor among hundreds in search algorithms. It does not guarantee high rankings on its own."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between a URL slug and a permalink?",
    answer: "A permalink is the complete, permanent URL of a specific page (e.g., 'https://creatorsfeel.com/tools/url-slug-generator'). The URL slug is only the final editable segment of that permalink ('url-slug-generator')."
  },
  {
    question: "How do URL slugs work in WordPress and other content management systems?",
    answer: "In WordPress and similar platforms, the system automatically drafts a default slug from your post title. You can customize this slug under the 'Permalink' panel before publishing to ensure it is clean, lowercase, and hyphen-separated."
  }
];
